er-level interfaces to edges; we show functions that extract the edge label, edge\nL302@P6: input arity, and edge input Node vectors (discarding the type information), both dependently-typed and\nL303@P6: existentially-typed with respect to the vector length. (The corresponding functions eOut etc.are not\nL304@P6-7: shown.)Wolfram Kahl 45\nL305@P7: record CodeGraph {m n : N} (inTypes : Vec Type m) (outTypes : Vec Type n) : Set1 where\nL306@P7: cgType : EdgeType\nL307@P7: cgType = record {inArity = m\nL308@P7: ; outArity = n\nL309@P7: ; inTypes = inTypes\nL310@P7: ; outTypes = outTypes}\nL311@P7: field Inner : Set\nL312@P7: iType : Inner → Type\nL313@P7: Node = Fin m ⊎ Inner\nL314@P7: nType : Node → Type\nL315@P7: nType = [(λ i → Vec.lookup i inTypes),iType]\nL316@P7: ′\nL317@P7: TypedNode : Type → Set\nL318@P7: TypedNode = Typed Node nType\nL319@P7: TypedNodes : {k : N} → Vec Type k → Set\nL320@P7: TypedNodes = TypedVec Node nType\nL321@P7: field output : TypedNodes outTypes\nL322@P7: input : TypedNodes inTypes\nL323@P7: input = λ i → (inj1 i,refl)\nL324@P7: field Edge : Set\nL325@P7: eInfo : Edge → EdgeInfo TypedNodes\nL326@P7: eLabel : Edge → EdgeLabel\nL327@P7: eLabel = EdgeInfo.eLab ◦ eInfo\nL328@P7: eInArity : Edge → N\nL329@P7: eInArity = EdgeInfo.inArity ◦ eInfo\nL330@P7: eIn : (e : Edge) → Vec Node (eInArity e)\nL331@P7: eIn e = mkVec (proj1 ◦ EdgeInfo.eInput(eInfo e))\nL332@P7: eIn′: Edge → Σ [k : N] (Vec Node k)\nL333@P7: eIn′e = eInArity e,eIn e\nL334@P7: Again, eOut is not guaranteed to reach all nodes, and, due to the possibility of multi-output operations,\nL335@P7: this cannot be amended by joining the Inner and Edge sets as in jungles. This and other degrees of\nL336@P7: generality contained in this definition can be useful for certain purposes, but also can be forbidden for\nL337@P7: other purposes by adding appropriate constraints.\nL338@P7: We show the function for producing primitive one-edge code graphs:\nL339@P7: prim : (l : EdgeLabel) → CodeGraph (EdgeLabel.inTypes l) (EdgeLabel.outTypes l)\nL340@P7: prim l = record\nL341@P7: {Inner = Fin (EdgeLabel.outArity l)\nL342@P7: ; output = λ i → (inj2 i,refl)\nL343@P7: ; Edge = ⊤\nL344@P7: ; eInfo = λ → record {eLab = l\nL345@P7: ; eInput = λ i → (inj1 i,refl)\nL346@P7: ; eOutput = λ i → (inj2 i,refl)}}\nL347@P7: While type-checking the three propositional equality proofs refl in here, Agda actually proves that the\nL348@P7-8: mentioned types are indeed equal: An Agda program can only produce CodeGraph values that are cor\u0002rectly typed, both on the external interface, and internally at each port of each edge.46 Dependently-Typed Formalisation of Typed Term Graphs\nL349@P8: 5 GS-Monoidal Categories\nL350@P8: Corradini and Gadducci proposed gs-monoidal categories for modelling acyclic term graphs [CG99];\nL351@P8: extended discussion of how code graphs fit into this framework is contained in [KAC06]. Here we only\nL352@P8: present a quick summary, and tie this into the formalisation in Sect. 3.\nL353@P8: In a category theory context, we write “f : A →B” to declare that morphism f goes from object A\nL354@P8: to object B, and use “.\nL355@P8: ,” as the associative binary composition operator; composition of two morphisms\nL356@P8: f : A →B and g : B′→C is defined iff B = B′, and then (f\nL357@P8: .\nL358@P8: , g) : A →C . Furthermore, the identity\nL359@P8: morphism for object A is written IA .\nL360@P8: Jungle can be seen to define morphisms of an untyped term graph category where objects are natural\nL361@P8: numbers. (For CodeGraph, the collection of Objects is Σ [k : N] (Vec Type k).)\nL362@P8: In the Jungle category, a morphism from m to n is an element of Jungle m n, that is, a term graph with\nL363@P8: m input nodes and n output nodes. More precisely, such a morphism is an isomorphism class of jungles,\nL364@P8: since node and edge identities do not matter; we will define a Setoid where the Carrier is Jungle m n and\nL365@P8: equivalence proofs are Jungle isomorphisms.\nL366@P8: Composition F\nL367@P8: .\nL368@P8: ,G “glues” together the output nodes of F with the respective input nodes of G, as we\nL369@P8: have implemented in seqJungle. The identity on n consists only of n input nodes which are also, in the\nL370@P8: same sequence, output nodes, and no edges, and is therefore constructed as a wiring graph:\nL371@P8: idJungle : {m : N} → Jungle m m\nL372@P8: idJungle = wire (allFin )\nL373@P8: Definition 5.1 A symmetric strict monoidal category [ML71] consists of a category C0, a strictly asso\u0002ciative monoidal bifunctor ⊗ with 1l as its strict unit, and a transformation X that associates with every\nL374@P8: two objects A and B an arrow XA ,B : A ⊗B→B ⊗ A with:\nL375@P8: (F ⊗G)\nL376@P8: .\nL377@P8: ,XC,D = XA ,B\nL378@P8: .\nL379@P8: ,(G⊗F) , XA ,B\nL380@P8: .\nL381@P8: , XB,A = IA ⊗IB ,\nL382@P8: XA ⊗B,C = (IA ⊗XB,C )\nL383@P8: .\nL384@P8: ,(XA ,C ⊗IB) , X1l,1l = I1l .\nL385@P8: For Jungle, the unit object 1l is the natural number 0, and ⊗ on objects is addition. On morphisms,\nL386@P8: ⊗ forms the disjoint union of code graphs, concatenating the input and output node sequences, as im\u0002plemented in parJungle. Xm,n differs from Im+n only in the fact that the two parts of the output node\nL387@P8: sequence are swapped:\nL388@P8: exchJungle : (m n : N) → Jungle (m + n) (n + m)\nL389@P8: exchJungle m n = wire (Vec.map (raise m) (allFin n)\nL390@P8: + Vec.map (inject+ n) (allFin m))\nL391@P8: Definition 5.2 A strict gs-monoidal category is a symmetric strict monoidal category where in addition\nL392@P8: ! associates with every object A of C0 an arrow !A : A →1l, and ∇ associates with every object A of\nL393@P8: C0 an arrow ∇A : A →A ⊗ A , such that I1l =!1l = ∇1l, and the following axioms hold:\nL394@P8: ∇A\nL395@P8: .\nL396@P8: ,(IA ⊗∇A ) = ∇A\nL397@P8: .\nL398@P8: ,(∇A ⊗IA ) ∇A\nL399@P8: .\nL400@P8: , XA ,A = ∇A ∇A\nL401@P8: .\nL402@P8: ,(IA ⊗!A ) = IA\nL403@P8: ∇A ⊗B\nL404@P8: .\nL405@P8: ,(IA ⊗XB,A ⊗IB) = ∇A ⊗∇B !A ⊗B =!A ⊗!B\nL406@P8: In Jungle, the “terminator” !n differs from In only in the fact that the output node sequence is empty.\nL407@P8: termJungle : {n : N} → Jungle n 0\nL408@P8-9: termJungle = wire [ ]Wolfram Kahl 47\nL409@P9: The “g” of “gs-monoidal” stands for “garbage”: all edges of a term graph G : m→n are garbage in the\nL410@P9: term graph G\nL411@P9: .\nL412@P9: ,!n.\nL413@P9: The duplicator ∇n in Jungle differs from In only in the fact that the output node sequence is the\nL414@P9: concatenation of the input node sequence with itself:\nL415@P9: dupJungle : {n : N} → Jungle n (n + n)\nL416@P9: dupJungle {n} = wire (allFin n + allFin n)\nL417@P9: The “s” of “gs-monoidal” stands for “sharing”: every input of ∇k\nL418@P9: .\nL419@P9: ,(F ⊗ G) is shared by F : k→m and\nL420@P9: G : k→n.\nL421@P9: Code graphs (and term graphs) over a fixed edge label set form a gs-monoidal category, but not a\nL422@P9: Cartesian category, where in addition ! and ∇ are natural transformations, i.e., for all F : A →B we\nL423@P9: have F\nL424@P9: .\nL425@P9: ,!B =!A and F\nL426@P9: .\nL427@P9: , ∇B = ∇A\nL428@P9: .\nL429@P9: ,(F ⊗F). To see how these naturality conditions are violated by term\nL430@P9: graphs, the following five Jungles correspond to the expressions below them (we draw jungles and code\nL431@P9: graphs from the inputs on top to the outputs at the bottom, with numbered triangles marking input and\nL432@P9: output positions, and rectangles enclosing edge labels).\nL433@P9: F\nL434@P9: 0\nL435@P9: 0 0\nL436@P9: F\nL437@P9: 0\nL438@P9: F\nL439@P9: 0 1\nL440@P9: 0\nL441@P9: F F\nL442@P9: 0 1\nL443@P9: 0\nL444@P9: F : 1→1 !1 F\nL445@P9: .\nL446@P9: ,!1 F\nL447@P9: .\nL448@P9: , ∇1 ∇1\nL449@P9: .\nL450@P9: ,(F ⊗F)\nL451@P9: Formalising (symmetric gs-) monoidal categories in Agda is a straight-forward extension of the standard\nL452@P9: type-theoretic formalisation of category theory deriving essentially from Kanda’s “effective categories”\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Type and Scope Safe Universe of Syntaxes with Binding: Their Semantics and Proofs (https://bentnib.org/binding-universe.pdf)\nciteturn99view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bentnib.org/binding-universe.pdf\",\"lineno\":935}); Total lines: 1363\nL883@P20: where rew = subst (ń a → J d a K _ _ _)\nL884@P20: Fig. 41. Zip: Characterising Structurally Equal Values with Related Substructures\nL885@P20: If we were to take a fixpoint of Zip, we could obtain a structural notion of equality for terms\nL886@P20: which we could prove equivalent to propositional equality. Although interesting in its own right,\nL887@P20: this section will focus on more advanced use-cases.\nL888@P20: 8.1 Simulation Lemma\nL889@P20: A Zip constraint appears naturally when we want to say that a semantics can simulate another one.\nL890@P20: Given a relation R\nL891@P20: V connecting values in V1 and V2, and a relation RC\nL892@P20: connecting computations\nL893@P20: in C1 and C2, we can define KripkeRrelating values Kripke V1 C1 and Kripke V2 C2 by stating\nL894@P20: that they send related inputs to related outputs. We use the relation transformer ∀[_] which lifts a\nL895@P20: relation on values to one on environments in a pointwise manner.\nL896@P20: We can then combine Zip and KripkeRto express the idea that two semantic objects of respective\nL897@P20: types J d K (Kripke V1 C1) and J d K (Kripke V2 C2) are synchronised. The simulation constraint on\nL898@P20: the algebras for two Semantics then becomes: given synchronized objects, the algebras should yield\nL899@P20-21: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.90:22 G. Allais, R. Atkey, J. Chapman, C. McBride, J. McKinna\nL900@P21: KripkeR: (∆ : List I) (τ : I) → [ Kripke V1 C1 ∆ τ →Û Kripke V2 C2 ∆ τ →Û κ Set ]\nL901@P21: KripkeR\nL902@P21: [] σ k1 k2 = R\nL903@P21: C k1 k2\nL904@P21: KripkeR ∆@(_ :: _) σ k1 k2 = ∀ th → ∀[ R\nL905@P21: V ] ρ1 ρ2 → RC\nL906@P21: (k1 th ρ1) (k2 th ρ2)\nL907@P21: Fig. 42. Relational Kripke Function Spaces: From Related Inputs to Related Outputs\nL908@P21: related computations. Together with self-explanatory constraints on var and thV, this constitutes\nL909@P21: the whole Simulation constraint:\nL910@P21: record Sim (d : Desc I) (S1 : Sem d V1 C1) (S2 : Sem d V2 C2) : Set where\nL911@P21: field thR\nL912@P21: : (σ : Thinning Γ ∆) → R\nL913@P21: V v1 v2 →\nL914@P21: R\nL915@P21: V (Sem.thV S1 v1 σ) (Sem.thV S2 v2 σ)\nL916@P21: varR: R\nL917@P21: V v1 v2 → RC\nL918@P21: (Sem.var S1 v1) (Sem.var S2 v2)\nL919@P21: algR: (b : J d K (Scope (Tm d s)) i Γ) → ∀[ R\nL920@P21: V ] ρ1 ρ2 →\nL921@P21: let v1 = fmap d (Sem.body S1 ρ1) b\nL922@P21: v2 = fmap d (Sem.body S2 ρ2) b\nL923@P21: in Zip d (KripkeR R\nL924@P21: V RC\nL925@P21: ) v1 v2 → R\nL926@P21: C\nL927@P21: (Sem.alg S1 v1) (Sem.alg S2 v2)\nL928@P21: Fig. 43. A Generic Notion of Simulation\nL929@P21: The fundamental lemma of simulations is a generic theorem showing that for each pair of\nL930@P21: Semantics respecting the Simulation constraint, we get related computations given environments\nL931@P21: of related input values. This theorem is once more mutually proven with a statement about Scopes,\nL932@P21: and Sizes play a crucial role in ensuring that the function is indeed total.\nL933@P21: sim : ∀[ R\nL934@P21: V ] ρ1 ρ2 → (t : Tm d s i Γ) → RC\nL935@P21: (Sem.sem S1 ρ1 t) (Sem.sem S2 ρ2 t)\nL936@P21: body : ∀[ R\nL937@P21: V ] ρ1 ρ2 → ∀ ∆ j → (t : Scope (Tm d s) ∆ j Γ) →\nL938@P21: KripkeR R\nL939@P21: V RC ∆ j (Sem.body S1 ρ1 ∆ j t) (Sem.body S2 ρ2 ∆ j t)\nL940@P21: Fig. 44. Fundamental Lemma of Simulations\nL941@P21: Instantiating this generic simulation lemma, we can for instance get that renaming and substi\u0002tution are extensional (given extensionally equal environments they produce syntactically equal\nL942@P21: terms), or that renaming is a special case of substitution. Of course these results are not new but\nL943@P21: having them generically over all syntaxes with binding is convenient; which we have experienced\nL944@P21: first hand when tackling the POPLMark Reloaded challenge where rensub was actually needed.\nL945@P21: rensub : (ρ : Thinning Γ ∆) (t : Tm d ∞ i Γ) → ren ρ t ≡ sub (‘var <$> ρ) t\nL946@P21: rensub ρ = Sim.sim RenSub (packR(ń _ → refl))\nL947@P21: Fig. 45. Renaming as a Substitution via Simulation\nL948@P21: When studying specific languages, new opportunities to deploy the fundamental lemma of\nL949@P21: simulations arise. Our solution to the POPLMark Reloaded challenge for instance describes the fact\nL950@P21-22: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.A Type and Scope Safe Universe of Syntaxes with Binding 90:23\nL951@P22: that sub ρ t reduces to sub ρ’ t whenever for all v, ρ(v) reduces to ρ’(v) as a Simulation. The main\nL952@P22: theorem (strong normalisation of STLC via a logical relation) is itself an instance of (the unary\nL953@P22: version of) the simulation lemma.\nL954@P22: The Simulation proof framework is the simplest examples of the abstract proof frameworks\nL955@P22: ACMM (2017) introduce. They also explain how a similar framework can be defined for fusion\nL956@P22: lemmas and deploy it for the renaming-substitution interactions but also their respective interactions\nL957@P22: with normalisation by evaluation. Now that we are familiarised with the techniques at hand, we\nL958@P22: can tackle this more complex example for all syntaxes definable in our framework.\nL959@P22: 8.2 Fusion Lemma\nL960@P22: Results which can be reformulated as the ability to fuse two traversals obtained as Semantics\nL961@P22: into one abound. When claiming that Tm is a Functor, we have to prove that two successive\nL962@P22: renamings can be fused into a single renaming where the Thinnings have been composed. Similarly,\nL963@P22: demonstrating that Tm is a relative Monad [Altenkirch et al. 2014] implies proving that two\nL964@P22: consecutive substitutions can be merged into a single one whose environment is the first one,\nL965@P22: where the second one has been applied in a pointwise manner. The Substitution Lemma central\nL966@P22: to most model constructions (see for instance [Mitchell and Moggi 1991]) states that a syntactic\nL967@P22: substitution followed by the evaluation of the resulting term into the model is equivalent to the\nL968@P22: evaluation of the original term with an environment corresponding to the evaluated substitution.\nL969@P22: A direct application of these results is our (to be published) entry to the POPLMark Reloaded\nL970@P22: challenge (2017). By using a Desc-based representation of intrinsically well typed and well scoped\nL971@P22: terms we directly inherit not only renaming and substitution but also all four fusion lemmas as\nL972@P22: corollaries of our generic results. This allows us to remove the usual boilerplate and go straight to\nL973@P22: the point. As all of these statements have precisely the same structure, we can once more devise a\nL974@P22: framework which will, provided that its constraints are satisfied, prove a generic fusion lemma.\nL975@P22: Fusion is more involved than simulation so we will step through each one of the constraints\nL976@P22: individually, trying to give the reader an intuition for why they are shaped the way they are.\nL977@P22: 8.2.1 The Fusion Constraints. The notion of fusion is defined for a triple of Semantics; each Si\nL978@P22: being defined for values in Vi and computations in Ci. The fundamental lemma associated to such\nL979@P22: a set of constraints will state that running S2 after S1 is equivalent to running S3 only.\nL980@P22: The definition of fusion is parametrised by three relations: R\nL981@P22: E\nL982@P22: relates triples of environments of\nL983@P22: values in (Γ śEnv) V1 ∆, (∆ śEnv) V2 Θ and (Γ śEnv) V3 Θ respectively; R\nL984@P22: V relates pairs of values\nL985@P22: V2 and V3; and R\nL986@P22: C\nL987@P22: , our notion of equivalence for evaluation results, relates pairs of computation\nL988@P22: in C2 and C3.\nL989@P22: The first obstacle we face is the formal definition of łrunning S2 after S1ž: for this statement to\nL990@P22: make sense, the result of running S1 ought to be a term. Or rather, we ought to be able to extract a\nL991@P22: term from a C1. Hence the first constraint: the existence of a quote1 function, which we supply as a\nL992@P22: field of the record Fusion. When dealing with syntactic semantics such as renaming or substitution\nL993@P22: this function will be the identity. However nothing prevents to try to prove for instance that\nL994@P22: normalisation by evaluation is idempotent in which case a bona fide reification function extracting\nL995@P22: terms from model values will be used.\nL996@P22: quote1 : (i : I) → [ C1 i →Û Tm d ∞ i ]\nL997@P22: Then, we have to think about what happens when going under a binder: S1 will produce a Kripke\nL998@P22: function space where a syntactic value is required. Provided that V1 is VarLike, we can make use\nL999@P22: of reify to get a Scope back. Hence the second constraint.\nL1000@P22: vlV1: VarLike V1\nL1001@P22-23: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.90:24 G. Allais, R. Atkey, J. Chapman, C. McBride, J. McKinna\nL1002@P23: Still thinking about going under binders: if three evaluation environments ρ1 in (Γ śEnv) V1 ∆,\nL1003@P23: ρ2 in (∆ śEnv) V2 Θ, and ρ3 in (Γ śEnv) V3 Θ are related by R\nL1004@P23: E\nL1005@P23: and we are given a thinning σ\nL1006@P23: from Θ to Ω then ρ1, the thinned ρ2 and the thinned ρ3 should still be related.\nL1007@P23: thR: (σ : Thinning Θ Ξ) → R\nL1008@P23: E\nL1009@P23: ρ1 ρ2 ρ3 →\nL1010@P23: R\nL1011@P23: E\nL1012@P23: ρ1 (thEnv (Sem.thV S2) ρ2 σ) (thEnv (Sem.thV S3) ρ3 σ)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://gallium.inria.fr/~huet/PUBLIC/zip.pdf)\nciteturn99view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gallium.inria.fr/~huet/PUBLIC/zip.pdf\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 206\nL143@P4: let go_up_memo (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL144@P4: Top -> failwith \"up of top\"\nL145@P4: | Node(left,p’,right) -> Loc(Siblings(left,t,right),p’);;\nL146@P4: let go_down_memo (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL147@P4: Item(_) -> failwith \"down of item\"\nL148@P4: | Siblings(left,t’,right) -> Loc(t’,Node(left,p,right));;\nL149@P4: We leave it to the reader to adapt other primitives.\nL150@P4: 2.2 First-order terms\nL151@P4: So far, our structures are completely untyped, our tree nodes are not even labelled.\nL152@P4: We have a kind of structured editor `a la LISP, but oriented more toward “splicing”\nL153@P4: operations than the usual rplaca and rplacd primitives.\nL154@P4: If we want to implement a tree-manipulation editor for abstract-syntax trees, we\nL155@P4: have to label our tree nodes with operator names. If we use items for this pur\u0002pose, this suggests the usual LISP encoding of first-order terms: F(T1, ..., Tn) being\nL156@P4: coded as the tree Section[Item(F); T1; ... Tn]. A dual solution is suggested by\nL157@P4: combinatory logic, where the comb-like structure respects the application ordering:\nL158@P4: [Tn; ... T1; Item(F)]. Neither of these solutions respects arity however.\nL159@P4: We shall not pursue details of such generic variations any more, but rather con\u0002sider how to adapt the idea to a specific given signature of operators given with\nL160@P4: their arities, in such a way that tree edition maintains well-formedness of the tree\nL161@P4: according to arities.\nL162@P4: Basically, to each constructor F of the signature with arity n we associate n path\nL163@P4: operators Node(F, i), with 1 ≤ i ≤ n, each of arity n, used when going down the\nL164@P4: i-th subtree of an F-term. More precisely, Node(F, i) has one path argument and\nL165@P4: n − 1 tree arguments holding the current siblings.\nL166@P4: We show for instance the structure corresponding to binary trees:\nL167@P4: type binary_tree =\nL168@P4: Nil\nL169@P4: | Cons of binary_tree * binary_tree;;\nL170@P4: type binary_path =\nL171@P4: Top\nL172@P4: | Left of binary_path * binary_tree\nL173@P4: | Right of binary_tree * binary_path;;\nL174@P4: type binary_location = Loc of binary_tree * binary_path;;\nL175@P4-5: let change (Loc(_,p)) t = Loc(t,p);;6 G´erard Huet\nL176@P5: let go_left (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL177@P5: Top -> failwith \"left of top\"\nL178@P5: | Left(father,right) -> failwith \"left of Left\"\nL179@P5: | Right(left,father) -> Loc(left,Left(father,t));;\nL180@P5: let go_right (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL181@P5: Top -> failwith \"right of top\"\nL182@P5: | Left(father,right) -> Loc(right,Right(t,father))\nL183@P5: | Right(left,father) -> failwith \"right of Right\";;\nL184@P5: let go_up (Loc(t,p)) = match p with\nL185@P5: Top -> failwith \"up of top\"\nL186@P5: | Left(father,right) -> Loc(Cons(t,right),father)\nL187@P5: | Right(left,father) -> Loc(Cons(left,t),father);;\nL188@P5: let go_first (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL189@P5: Nil -> failwith \"first of Nil\"\nL190@P5: | Cons(left,right) -> Loc(left,Left(p,right));;\nL191@P5: let go_second (Loc(t,p)) = match t with\nL192@P5: Nil -> failwith \"second of Nil\"\nL193@P5: | Cons(left,right) -> Loc(right,Right(left,p));;\nL194@P5: Efficient destructive algorithms on binary trees may be programmed with these\nL195@P5: completely applicative primitives, which all use constant time, since they all reduce\nL196@P5: to local pointer manipulation.\nL197@P5: References\nL198@P5: V. Donzeau-Gouge, G. Huet, G. Kahn, B. Lang. “Programming Environments based on\nL199@P5: Structured Editors: The MENTOR Experience.” In Interactive Programming En\u0002vironments. Eds. Barstow D., Shrobe H. and Sandewall E., McGraw Hill, 1984, pp.\nL200@P5: 128-140.\nL201@P5: G. Huet. “Confluent Reductions: Abstract Properties and Applications to Term Rewriting\nL202@P5: Systems.” J. Assoc. Comp. Mach. 27,4 (1980) 797–821.\nL203@P5: X. Leroy, D. R´emy, J. Vouillon. “The Objective Caml system, documenta\u0002tion and user’s manual - release 1.02.” INRIA, oct. 1996. Available on\nL204@P5: ftp.inria.fr:INRIA/Projects/cristal.\nL205@P5: L. C. Paulson. “ML for the working programmer.” Cambridge University Press, 1991.\n"
